### What is the problem?

Given the root of a binary tree, determine whether it is a valid binary search tree (BST). A valid BST requires that every node's value is strictly greater than all values in its left subtree and strictly less than all values in its right subtree.

```java
package com.bank.tree;

public class ValidateBST {

    static class TreeNode {
        int val;
        TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int val) { this.val = val; }
    }

    public static boolean isValidBST(TreeNode root) {
        return validate(root, Long.MIN_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE);
    }

    private static boolean validate(TreeNode node, long low, long high) {
        if (node == null) return true;
        if (node.val <= low || node.val >= high) return false;
        return validate(node.left, low, node.val)
            && validate(node.right, node.val, high);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        //       50000
        //      /     \
        //   30000   70000
        //   /   \       \
        // 20000 40000  80000
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(50000);
        root.left = new TreeNode(30000);
        root.right = new TreeNode(70000);
        root.left.left = new TreeNode(20000);
        root.left.right = new TreeNode(40000);
        root.right.right = new TreeNode(80000);

        System.out.println("Valid BST: " + isValidBST(root));
    }
}
```

```text
Valid BST: true
```

### Why is checking only immediate children wrong?

The common mistake is verifying only that a node is greater than its left child and less than its right child. This misses violations where a deep node satisfies its parent but violates an ancestor's bound.

```java
package com.bank.tree;

public class NaiveCheckFails {

    static class TreeNode {
        int val;
        TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int v) { this.val = v; }
    }

    // WRONG: checks only immediate children
    static boolean naiveCheck(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) return true;
        if (node.left != null && node.left.val >= node.val) return false;
        if (node.right != null && node.right.val <= node.val) return false;
        return naiveCheck(node.left) && naiveCheck(node.right);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        //       50000
        //      /     \
        //   30000   70000
        //       \
        //      60000  <-- violates 50000's left subtree constraint!
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(50000);
        root.left = new TreeNode(30000);
        root.right = new TreeNode(70000);
        root.left.right = new TreeNode(60000);

        System.out.println("Naive check: " + naiveCheck(root));  // true (WRONG)
        System.out.println("Correct:     " + isValidBST(root));  // false
    }

    static boolean isValidBST(TreeNode root) {
        return validate(root, Long.MIN_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE);
    }

    static boolean validate(TreeNode n, long lo, long hi) {
        if (n == null) return true;
        if (n.val <= lo || n.val >= hi) return false;
        return validate(n.left, lo, n.val) && validate(n.right, n.val, hi);
    }
}
```

```text
Naive check: true
Correct:     false
```

### How do the bounds tighten during recursion?

The root starts with bounds (negative infinity, positive infinity). When you go left, the current value becomes the new upper bound because everything in the left subtree must be smaller. When you go right, the current value becomes the new lower bound because everything in the right subtree must be larger.

```java
package com.bank.tree;

public class BoundsTrace {

    static class TreeNode {
        int val;
        TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int v) { this.val = v; }
    }

    static boolean validateTrace(TreeNode node, long lo, long hi, String path) {
        if (node == null) return true;
        System.out.println(path + "node=" + node.val
            + " bounds=(" + lo + ", " + hi + ")"
            + (node.val <= lo || node.val >= hi ? " VIOLATION" : " OK"));
        if (node.val <= lo || node.val >= hi) return false;
        return validateTrace(node.left, lo, node.val, path + "L->")
            && validateTrace(node.right, node.val, hi, path + "R->");
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(50000);
        root.left = new TreeNode(30000);
        root.right = new TreeNode(70000);
        root.left.left = new TreeNode(20000);
        root.left.right = new TreeNode(40000);
        validateTrace(root, Long.MIN_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE, "");
    }
}
```

```text
node=50000 bounds=(-9223372036854775808, 9223372036854775807) OK
L->node=30000 bounds=(-9223372036854775808, 50000) OK
L->L->node=20000 bounds=(-9223372036854775808, 30000) OK
L->R->node=40000 bounds=(30000, 50000) OK
R->node=70000 bounds=(50000, 9223372036854775807) OK
```

### Why use Long instead of Integer for bounds?

If the tree contains `Integer.MIN_VALUE` or `Integer.MAX_VALUE` as node values, using `int` bounds causes false failures because the node value would equal the sentinel. Using `long` sentinels avoids this edge case.

```java
package com.bank.tree;

public class LongBoundsEdge {
    static class TreeNode {
        int val;
        TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int v) { this.val = v; }
    }

    // Using int bounds: FAILS for Integer.MIN_VALUE node
    static boolean validateInt(TreeNode n, int lo, int hi) {
        if (n == null) return true;
        if (n.val <= lo || n.val >= hi) return false;
        return validateInt(n.left, lo, n.val) && validateInt(n.right, n.val, hi);
    }

    // Using long bounds: CORRECT
    static boolean validateLong(TreeNode n, long lo, long hi) {
        if (n == null) return true;
        if (n.val <= lo || n.val >= hi) return false;
        return validateLong(n.left, lo, n.val) && validateLong(n.right, n.val, hi);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(Integer.MIN_VALUE);
        System.out.println("Int bounds:  " + validateInt(root, Integer.MIN_VALUE, Integer.MAX_VALUE));
        System.out.println("Long bounds: " + validateLong(root, Long.MIN_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE));
    }
}
```

```text
Int bounds:  false
Long bounds: true
```

### What is the inorder traversal alternative?

An inorder traversal of a valid BST produces values in strictly increasing order. You can validate the tree by performing an inorder traversal and checking that each value is strictly greater than the previous one.

```java
package com.bank.tree;

public class InorderValidation {

    static class TreeNode {
        int val;
        TreeNode left, right;
        TreeNode(int v) { this.val = v; }
    }

    private static long prev = Long.MIN_VALUE;

    public static boolean isValidBSTInorder(TreeNode node) {
        if (node == null) return true;
        if (!isValidBSTInorder(node.left)) return false;
        if (node.val <= prev) return false;
        prev = node.val;
        return isValidBSTInorder(node.right);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TreeNode root = new TreeNode(50000);
        root.left = new TreeNode(30000);
        root.right = new TreeNode(70000);
        prev = Long.MIN_VALUE;
        System.out.println("Valid: " + isValidBSTInorder(root));
    }
}
```

```text
Valid: true
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Metric | Bounds Approach | Inorder Approach |
|---|---|---|
| Time | O(n) | O(n) |
| Space | O(h) recursion stack | O(h) recursion stack |
| Early termination | Yes, on first violation | Yes, on first violation |
| Extra state | None (bounds are parameters) | Previous value variable |

### How is this used in a banking system?

At the National Bank of India, account records are indexed in a BST by account number for fast lookup. After a bulk insert, the tree must be validated. A single misplaced record silently breaks every future lookup. The bounds-based validation catches exactly the dangerous case where a record satisfies its immediate parent but violates an ancestor's range.

```java
package com.bank.tree;

public class BankAccountBST {

    static class AccountNode {
        long accountNumber;
        String name;
        AccountNode left, right;
        AccountNode(long num, String name) {
            this.accountNumber = num;
            this.name = name;
        }
    }

    static boolean validateAccounts(AccountNode node, long lo, long hi) {
        if (node == null) return true;
        if (node.accountNumber <= lo || node.accountNumber >= hi) {
            System.out.println("INVALID: " + node.accountNumber
                + " outside (" + lo + ", " + hi + ")");
            return false;
        }
        return validateAccounts(node.left, lo, node.accountNumber)
            && validateAccounts(node.right, node.accountNumber, hi);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        AccountNode root = new AccountNode(50000, "Rajesh");
        root.left = new AccountNode(30000, "Priya");
        root.right = new AccountNode(70000, "Pankaj");
        root.left.right = new AccountNode(60000, "Invalid"); // violates!

        boolean valid = validateAccounts(root, Long.MIN_VALUE, Long.MAX_VALUE);
        System.out.println("Account tree valid: " + valid);
    }
}
```

```text
INVALID: 60000 outside (-9223372036854775808, 50000)
Account tree valid: false
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers frequently ask:
- Can you do it iteratively? (Yes, use a stack with bounds tuples.)
- What if duplicates are allowed on one side? (Adjust the comparison to allow equality on the chosen side.)
- How do you find the lowest common ancestor in a BST? (Use the BST property to decide left or right.)
- How do you convert a sorted array to a balanced BST? (Pick the middle element as root, recurse on halves.)
